WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.864 --> 00:00:04.533
Line 3 must make enough cartons for the customer in a shift.

2
00:00:05.565 --> 00:00:08.445
The module Capacity and flow is about how much a

3
00:00:08.514 --> 00:00:11.034
line can make and why it does not always work.

4
00:00:12.108 --> 00:00:13.304
You are the engineer.

5
00:00:14.033 --> 00:00:17.563
With the team lead you keep the line on pace for a whole shift.

6
00:00:18.563 --> 00:00:20.757
First the pace and what limits the line,

7
00:00:20.874 --> 00:00:24.485
with takt and line balance and with bottleneck and capacity.

8
00:00:25.573 --> 00:00:31.111
Then where the minutes go, with OEE and losses and with changeover and batch size.

9
00:00:32.422 --> 00:00:35.092
The first topic is Takt and line balance.

10
00:00:35.777 --> 00:00:38.482
Takt time is the pace the customer asks for.

11
00:00:39.527 --> 00:00:42.708
Line balancing divides the work over the stations,

12
00:00:42.952 --> 00:00:45.645
so each station sits just under takt.

13
00:00:46.687 --> 00:00:49.369
You draw the work per station in a yamazumi.

14
00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.446
The second topic is Bottleneck and capacity.

15
00:00:54.196 --> 00:00:56.994
A line never makes more than its slowest step.

16
00:00:58.047 --> 00:01:01.321
Capacity is what the line can really make after losses.

17
00:01:02.035 --> 00:01:03.951
Plan on that, not on the paper.

18
00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:07.800
You fix the hours of the line in a shift model.

19
00:01:09.085 --> 00:01:11.721
The third topic is OEE and losses.

20
00:01:12.501 --> 00:01:16.588
OEE measures how much planned time goes into good parts.

21
00:01:17.594 --> 00:01:21.809
You lose the rest to the six big losses, from breakdowns to rejects.

22
00:01:22.904 --> 00:01:24.843
OEE does not say why.

23
00:01:25.701 --> 00:01:29.648
On the board you note time, duration and cause in a downtime log.

24
00:01:30.961 --> 00:01:33.829
The fourth topic is Changeover and batch size.

25
00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:37.181
A changeover is switching to another product.

26
00:01:38.213 --> 00:01:42.079
The batch size is the number of parts between two changeovers.

27
00:01:42.806 --> 00:01:45.186
A changeover costs time without output.

28
00:01:46.254 --> 00:01:49.423
You put the tools ready beforehand on a changeover cart.

29
00:01:50.487 --> 00:01:55.224
That is how you and the team lead can keep line 3 on pace for the whole shift.

30
00:01:56.562 --> 00:01:57.340
The whole map.

31
00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.097
The first station is Capacity and flow, in Keeping the plant running.

32
00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.314
Each of the four topics has four levels.

33
00:02:06.323 --> 00:02:09.922
Start at Basics level with takt, cycle and lead time.

34
00:02:10.603 --> 00:02:13.285
The customer’s pace is the yardstick for the rest.

35
00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:17.009
At Working knowledge level come line balancing,

36
00:02:17.183 --> 00:02:20.318
utilisation, the Pareto and the SMED workshop.

37
00:02:21.364 --> 00:02:26.066
At Advanced level come precedence and variation, five focusing steps,

38
00:02:26.264 --> 00:02:29.201
defining OEE honestly and batch size.

39
00:02:30.221 --> 00:02:33.576
At Expert level come staffing, an hour on the bottleneck,

40
00:02:33.832 --> 00:02:36.235
OEE in money and the product wheel.

41
00:02:37.244 --> 00:02:41.296
Every lesson and every film in this module has a place on this map.
